WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:06.350
Some people think hospitals are the most dangerous
places on earth, more people die inside them

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.340
than anywhere else.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.830
When people say things like that, they are
technically right and may get fooled by what’s

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:21.019
known as selection bias.

00:00:21.019 --> 00:00:26.170
A selection bias occurs when we look at information
that it is not fully representative of the

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.560
data intended to be studied.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:35.180
As a result of the biased sample, we then
draw a false conclusion.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:41.370
One story that explains the bias perhaps better
than any other is that of Abraham Wald.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:47.000
Wald was a brilliant mathematician, who, after
the Nazis persecuted him and his family as

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.920
Jews in Austria, fled to the United States
in 1938.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.550
During World War II, Wald was invited to become
a member of the Statistical Research Group,

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:05.320
an elite think tank to aid the American war
effort against Nazi Germany.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.030
One day the US Air Force came to Wald and
his colleagues with a problem.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:15.710
Many of their planes got shot down due to
a lack of armor.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:20.750
The officers presented Wald with data for
all the aircrafts that made it back from their

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.750
mission.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:28.740
The planes had lots of holes on the body and
wings but less below the engines.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.900
The officers then asked the mathematicians
to compute the optimal protection by concentrating

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.340
the armor where the planes were getting hit
the most.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.350
After studying the problem, Wald suggested
something unexpected.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.119
The armor, he said, doesn’t go where the
bullet holes are.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.500
It goes where the bullet holes aren’t.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:56.110
The officers didn’t understand, because
they were looking at a biased sample.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.140
Wald wasn’t.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.610
He realized that to get representative data
to analyze, he needed to include the missing

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:08.470
holes, the missing planes, the missing information.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.849
The reason planes were coming back with fewer
hits to the engine is that planes that got

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.510
hit in the engine weren’t coming back, he
explained.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:22.480
But selection bias isn’t just the result
of missing information.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:27.800
The simpson paradox is a phenomenon in which
a trend appears in groups of data but then

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.910
when the groups are combined, disappears.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:38.120
It shows the importance of really understanding
the data we select for analysis.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:43.450
One famous example came from students applying
to the University of California, Berkeley

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.769
in 1973.

00:02:45.769 --> 00:02:51.140
The data showed that males applying were more
likely to be accepted than females.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.800
People thought that the institution was discriminating
against women.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.470
When researchers dug deeper into the data
they found out that men had applied to less

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.510
competitive departments with higher rates
of admission.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:10.310
Women chose more competitive departments with
fewer available spots.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.220
After correcting for this detail the data
showed a significant bias in favor of women

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:19.040
- not men.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:22.230
Planes that were shot in the engines were
not analyzed.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:28.269
Women in Berkley weren’t discriminated against,
but instead picked more competitive classes.

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:33.690
People that die in hospitals are often already
sick when they are admitted.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:36.150
What are your thoughts?

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.569
Is selection bias corrupting your decision
making?

00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:44.200
And if it’s fooling you, how about the people
behind the “research” you see being published

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.599
in popular media?

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:51.340
Did they really make sure they selected an
unbiased fully representative sample?

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.840
Share your thoughts in the comments below!

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.239
And if you still don’t quite understand
it, here is a simple challenge to experience

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:01.890
it first hand!

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.730
Go out of your house, knock at the doors of
your next 10 neighbors, and ask those who

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.030
open if they are afraid of strangers.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.981
After you are done, report your findings in
the comments below and explain to us: what

00:04:14.981 --> 00:04:20.540
can your research tell us about your community?

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:24.100
Anything?

